<?php

return [
    [
        'slug' => 'why-penangs-chip-boom-is-finally-reaching-the-factory-floor',
        'desk' => 'Business',
        'title' => 'Why Penang’s chip boom is finally reaching the factory floor',
        'deck' => 'After years of investment headlines, the money is starting to show up in pay slips, training rooms and the canteen queue.',
        'byline' => 'By the Meridian business desk',
        'long_read' => false,
        'sections' => [
            ['name' => 'The night shift', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'At ten minutes to eleven, the car park at Jelutong Testworks in Bayan Lepas fills with motorcycles going in both directions. The day shift is leaving, the night shift is arriving, and for a few minutes everybody is trying to use the same gate. Kavitha Raman, 31, parks under the one working lamp, takes off her helmet and checks her phone for the thing she has been checking all week: her new pay slip.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She started here nine years ago as a line operator, placing chips into test sockets by hand. She is now a test technician, in charge of four machines the size of wardrobes, each worth more than her family’s house in Sungai Petani. Last month her basic pay went up for the third time in two years.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“For a long time I read in the newspaper that Penang was booming,” she says. “I was happy for Penang. I wanted to meet it.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Penang has been a chip town since the free trade zone opened at Bayan Lepas in the early 1970s. For most of that time, the big announcements have happened at one altitude and the wages at another. The investment figures of the past few years have been large enough to embarrass a thesaurus. Workers at the smaller suppliers mostly heard about them from relatives asking for a loan.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'That is starting to shift. The change is uneven and slower than the ribbon-cutting suggests. But around Bayan Lepas, Batu Kawan and Bukit Minyak, you can now see it in pay slips, in training rooms and in the length of the lunch queue.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Follow the money', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Lim Soo Hoon, 56, runs Jelutong Testworks, which her father started in a rented unit with six staff and a borrowed forklift. It now employs about 380 people and tests chips for customers it is contractually forbidden to name. She is cheerful about this. “Half my clients are a code on an invoice,” she says.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'For decades, she says, firms like hers lived on the thin end of the business. The giants designed and built. The suppliers did the repetitive work, and competed on price, which meant competing on wages. A pay rise at a small subcontractor was something you discussed after the Chinese New Year orders had been paid for, and then you discussed it some more.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Two things have changed that. The work has become harder, as chips are stacked and packaged in ways that leave less room for error. And the people who can do that work have become scarce, because every new plant on the island and across the bridge is hiring from the same pool.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“The big factories come with their own engineers,” Lim says. “What they don’t bring is somebody who can fix a conveyor in Bukit Minyak at three in the morning.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Technicians now know their price. Ooi Wei Jie, who runs a small labour-market consultancy called Selat Analytics, has spent three years collecting job advertisements from about 40 Penang suppliers. By his count, the typical advertised pay for an experienced technician has risen by about a fifth over that period. Operators have seen much less. “The boom has arrived,” he says. “It has come in through the maintenance door.”'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Learning the machine', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Lim’s answer to the poaching has been to grow her own. A former storeroom at the back of the plant is now a classroom with eight desks, a whiteboard and a retired tester with its panels taken off so that students can see inside. Operators who pass a 14-week course, taken mostly on rest days, move up a grade and get a raise. So far 61 have finished it.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Azlan Hashim, 44, was one of the first. He spent 20 years on the line and assumed, reasonably, that the robots were coming for him. He signed up mainly to find out how long he had.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“Now I’m the one who tells the robot it’s wrong,” he says. “My wife tells people I’m in management. I’m not. I have a better torch.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The classroom has also had an effect that nobody planned. Operators who used to treat the test floor as a place for men with degrees now turn up on their day off to argue about error codes. Three of the last intake were mothers who did their problem sets in the canteen during breaks and, by Azlan’s account, finished them sooner than he did. One has since been made a shift lead. Her former supervisor now reports to her, and they have agreed not to discuss it.'],
                ['type' => 'guide', 'title' => 'A short guide to the factory floor', 'rows' => [
                    ['term' => 'Front end', 'meaning' => 'Making the chip on a silicon wafer. Very little of this happens in Penang'],
                    ['term' => 'Back end', 'meaning' => 'Cutting, packaging and testing the finished chips. Penang’s speciality'],
                    ['term' => 'Advanced packaging', 'meaning' => 'Stacking or joining several chips so they work as one'],
                    ['term' => 'Burn-in', 'meaning' => 'Running chips hot for hours to catch the weak ones early'],
                    ['term' => 'Yield', 'meaning' => 'The share of chips that pass. The number everybody is shouted at about'],
                ]],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'There is a hard-headed reason for the classroom. Training a technician in-house costs Lim less than losing one and hiring another, and a worker who owes his promotion to the company tends to think twice about a RM300 offer from across the road. Some still leave. She has stopped taking it personally, and started sending the good ones a card.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Who gets left', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'pullquote', 'text' => 'I was happy for Penang. I wanted to meet it.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The gains stop at a fairly clear line. Rohani Yusof, 52, has worked on assembly lines for 30 years. Her eyes can no longer manage the microscope work, so she has moved to packing, where the pay has barely changed. She doesn’t resent the younger technicians. She would like somebody to explain what the plan is for her.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Migrant workers sit further down still. Bishnu Thapa, 28, came from Nepal on a three-year contract, and his pay was settled before he left home. He has heard about the raises. He has also noticed that he works beside the people getting them.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“We share the same machine,” he says. “On the pay slip, we are in different countries.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'And some of the extra money is leaving as fast as it comes. Kavitha’s room in Bayan Baru has gone up twice since she moved in, because the landlord reads the same newspapers she does. Colleagues have moved to the mainland for cheaper rent, and pay for it on the bridge, where the morning crawl is now long enough to finish a podcast and start regretting it.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Ooi thinks the next two years will decide how far the money travels. “Technician pay moved because technicians were short,” he says. “Nobody has been short of operators yet. Once automation takes more of the line, they may never be.”'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'The canteen index', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Zaiton Mahmud, 61, has sold nasi lemak from a stall outside the industrial park’s third gate since the days when the buyers paid in coins. She keeps no accounts, and she has a more reliable indicator than most economists.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“Before, they took egg,” she says. “Now they take egg and sambal sotong. On pay day, some take chicken and don’t even ask how much.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'At half past six, the night shift starts coming out. Kavitha buys a packet with sotong and sits on the low wall by the gate. Her sister, who is 19 and in Sungai Petani, has asked about the course in the storeroom. Kavitha has told her to apply, and to bring a jacket, because the air conditioning is the one thing at the plant that has always been generous.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She has finally seen the pay slip. It went up by RM240. She is saving most of it, and she has plans for the rest, which involve a better helmet. Penang’s boom, she says, is now close enough to wave at.'],
            ]],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug' => 'flood-season-explained-what-has-changed-since-the-2021-floods',
        'desk' => 'Nation',
        'title' => 'Flood season, explained: what has changed since the 2021 floods',
        'deck' => 'Five years on, the warnings are quicker and the drains are wider, and the low ground is still exactly where it was.',
        'byline' => 'By the Meridian nation desk',
        'long_read' => false,
        'sections' => [
            ['name' => 'The gatepost', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'There is a line of red paint on Roslan Ismail’s gatepost, a little above the height of his shoulder. He put it there in January 2022, once the mud had been shovelled out and the smell had started to leave. Next to it he wrote the date in marker pen. Every year since, he has repainted the line before the rains, the way other men wash the car before Hari Raya.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Roslan, 66, is a retired land surveyor. He lives in Taman Seri Bayu, an estate of about 400 terrace houses on the flat ground of the Klang Valley, a short walk from a river most residents could not have named before December 2021. They can all name it now.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“People say that was a once-in-a-lifetime flood,” he says. “I’m a surveyor. I would like to see the working.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'It is November, and the monsoon has started. So this is a good moment to ask a plain question. Nearly five years after one of the worst floods the Klang Valley has known, what is actually different? We spent three weeks in and around Taman Seri Bayu, talking to the people who live there and to the engineers they now phone. The short answer is that a good deal has changed, and most of it is in how people behave.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'What happened', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In the middle of December 2021, days of heavy rain from a slow-moving tropical depression fell across the peninsula. Selangor and Kuala Lumpur were among the worst hit. Rivers burst their banks, main roads turned into channels, and whole neighbourhoods went under within hours. Dozens of people died and tens of thousands had to leave their homes.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The shock came partly from the location. Malaysians know about floods. Kelantan, Terengganu and Pahang go through them most years, during the northeast monsoon that runs from about November to March. The west coast cities tended to think of flooding as a thing that happened on television, and to worry instead about flash floods that closed one road for one afternoon.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In Taman Seri Bayu, the water came up in the dark. Aishah Zulkifli, 41, remembers carrying the rice cooker upstairs, because it seemed important at the time. Her neighbours spent a night on a roof. A boat arrived the following afternoon, rowed by two teenagers from the next estate who had borrowed it from someone who had not been consulted.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Roslan’s own survey of the estate, done street by street with a borrowed level, found that the water stood more than a metre and a half deep at the low end. Nobody asked him to do this. He has the results in a ring binder, and he will show you.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Why rain floods', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Priya Nair, an independent hydrologist who now advises several residents’ groups, starts her talks with a kitchen sink. A sink floods for one of three reasons. Too much is coming out of the tap, the drain is too small, or something is blocking the pipe further down. In a bad flood, she says, it is usually all three.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Too much water is the easy part to understand. In a warmer atmosphere, the heaviest bursts of rain tend to get heavier, and the 2021 storm dropped more than a usual month’s rain on parts of Selangor in a very short time.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The drain is the part people can argue about. Forest and open ground soak rain up and release it slowly. Roofs, roads and car parks send it straight to the river. A drain sized for the Klang Valley of 1985 now serves a very different place, and every new township upstream sends its water down to somebody else’s township.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The blockage is often the sea. The lower Klang River is affected by tides, so a high tide can slow the river’s escape just when it most needs to leave. “You can’t dredge the moon,” Nair says. Rubbish also plays its part, and she has a slide of a sofa wedged in a culvert that gets a laugh from every audience except the one that lives near that culvert.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Some of this is beyond any council. Nair is careful about what she promises, and she avoids the word “prevent”. Her preferred word is “shorter”: a shorter flood, a shallower one, one that gives people longer to get out.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'The warning chain', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The clearest improvement is in warnings. In 2021, many people in Taman Seri Bayu learned about the flood from the flood. Today, river level readings are easy to find on a phone, alerts travel faster, and most low-lying neighbourhoods have somebody who is obsessively watching them.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In Taman Seri Bayu that somebody is Aishah, a school bookkeeper. She runs a 380-member group chat with one strict rule: during heavy rain, only river levels, road closures and missing persons. Good morning stickers result in removal. She has removed her own uncle.'],
                ['type' => 'guide', 'title' => 'What the alert words mean', 'rows' => [
                    ['term' => 'Normal', 'meaning' => 'The river is at its usual level'],
                    ['term' => 'Waspada', 'meaning' => 'Alert. The river is rising. Keep watching'],
                    ['term' => 'Amaran', 'meaning' => 'Warning. Close to overflowing. Move the car and pack a bag'],
                    ['term' => 'Bahaya', 'meaning' => 'Danger. Flooding is likely. Leave when you are told to, or sooner'],
                    ['term' => 'PPS', 'meaning' => 'Pusat pemindahan sementara, a temporary relief centre, often a school or hall'],
                ]],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Her list has a second page, which matters more. It names every house with someone who cannot climb stairs or walk far, and who will collect them. There are 23 such houses. Each has two named neighbours, in case one is away, and the pairings are checked every October. The system cost nothing and took about a weekend to set up.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“The warning is only useful if somebody goes to knock on the door,” she says. “Auntie Lee doesn’t have a group chat. Auntie Lee has me.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Practice has changed too. Each October the estate holds a drill of sorts, which mostly consists of people walking to the higher ground by the mosque and finding out how long that takes with a grandmother, two cats and a box of documents. Last year’s best time was 19 minutes. The worst was a family who stopped to argue about the cats. Aishah writes down every result, and she has started asking the slowest houses to go as soon as the word Amaran appears.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Drains and ponds', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The physical work is real, and slower. Across the Klang Valley, crews have widened channels, dredged river beds and built or enlarged ponds that hold water back during a storm. Anyone who drives through Selangor has sat behind the excavators. Taman Seri Bayu’s main monsoon drain was deepened in 2024, and its concrete walls are the newest thing in the neighbourhood.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Residents approve, within limits. Roslan has measured the new drain, naturally, and says it should cope with a heavy storm far better than the old one did. He also points out that it empties into the same river, at the same height, as before. A larger pipe into a full river gives you a very efficient way of waiting.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Nair says this is why the less exciting measures count for so much. A retention pond needs regular desilting, or it slowly becomes a field with a fence around it. A pump needs diesel and somebody who has the key. Flood defences tend to get opened by important people and maintained by nobody in particular.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Planning is the slowest piece. Land that flooded in 2021 has been sold and built on since. Developers point out that their projects include drainage, and many do. Nair’s view is that each project can pass its own test while the valley as a whole fails. “Everybody’s homework is correct,” she says. “The class is still underwater.”'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Who pays', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The change residents talk about most, once you get them going, is money. After the flood, many families in the estate discovered that their car insurance did not cover flood damage unless they had paid a small extra amount for it, and that their fire policy on the house did not cover it either.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Daniel Chew, 47, an insurance agent in nearby Klang, says that his phone rang all through January 2022 with people asking about policies they had never read. Flood cover on cars is now, in his experience, the first thing people ask about. Home contents are another matter. Few of his customers in low-lying areas carry enough cover to replace what they lost, and he doubts that premiums for those postcodes will get any kinder.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'So people have adapted in cheaper ways. Walk through Taman Seri Bayu and you will see power sockets moved up to waist height, kitchen cabinets on legs, and steel flood boards that slot in across the gates. A local welder, Muthu Arumugam, reckons he has built about 200 of those boards. He describes the flood, with some embarrassment, as the best thing that has happened to the business.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Some families have simply gone. Aishah counts 31 houses that have changed hands since 2021. The buyers tend to be younger and to pay less. Few of them, she says, asked about the red line on the gatepost down the road.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Before the rain', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'So what should someone in a low-lying area do this month? The advice from the people we spoke to was unglamorous and consistent. Know your river’s name and where to find its level. Photograph your documents and keep the originals upstairs or in a waterproof bag. Move the car early, since a car costs more than an awkward conversation with the security guard at the multi-storey.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Find out where your relief centre is before you need it, and how you would get there if the main road were closed. Turn off the electricity at the mains before water reaches the sockets. Never walk or drive through moving water, however shallow it looks. And find out who on your street cannot get out alone.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'None of this will stop a flood. Nobody in Taman Seri Bayu believes that anything will, at least in the time they plan to go on living there. What they have now is about two hours more warning than last time, by Aishah’s estimate, and a clear idea of what to do with it.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'On a damp Saturday, Roslan is repainting his line. It takes about four minutes. His grandson holds the tin, and asks why they do this when there is no water. Roslan tells him that this is the best time for it. Then he stands back and checks that it is level.'],
            ]],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug' => 'the-quiet-return-of-the-neighbourhood-kopitiam',
        'desk' => 'Food',
        'title' => 'The quiet return of the neighbourhood kopitiam',
        'deck' => 'A younger generation is taking over the corner coffee shop, and trying very hard not to change it.',
        'byline' => 'By the Meridian food desk',
        'long_read' => false,
        'sections' => [
            ['name' => 'The first pour', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'At seven on a Tuesday morning, the loudest thing in Kedai Kopi Lam Fatt is the kettle. Uncle Keong, 71, pours from shoulder height into a cloth filter that has gone the colour of old teak. He has made coffee this way for 48 years. Beside him, his niece Mei Ling has propped her phone against the sugar tin and is filming his hands.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The video is not for social media. She is trying to write down the recipe, because he has never measured anything.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“He says ‘until it smells right’,” she says. “That is not a recipe. That is a threat.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Mei Ling, 34, left a job in Kuala Lumpur last year to take over the shop. She is part of a small, unannounced movement across Ipoh, Penang and the older parts of Klang: children and grandchildren coming back to the corner coffee shops their families were about to close.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'The handover', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'For a long time, the story of the kopitiam ran in one direction. The owners grew old. Their children became accountants. The building was sold, and a phone shop or a bubble tea chain moved in. The marble tables ended up in somebody’s cafe in Bangsar, priced as antiques.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'What has changed is harder to pin down than a trend. Some of the returners were pushed, by a retrenchment or a parent’s illness. Others did the sums on a city salary and a city rent, and decided that a paid-off shop lot was worth more than either.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“I thought I was coming home for six months,” says Farid, 29, who now runs his late grandfather’s shop with his sister in a quiet row near the old railway line. “Then I saw the regulars. Nobody told me that you inherit the people too.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Inheriting the people turns out to be the hard part. Regulars have opinions. When Mei Ling moved the cashier’s table a metre to the left, three customers complained in a single morning. She moved it back.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Farid also inherited his grandfather’s ledger of who owes what. Some of the debts date from 1994. He has no plans to collect. On slow afternoons he reads the names aloud, and now and then somebody at the next table says that man was his father.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'What stays', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The new owners are careful about what they touch. Walk into any of these shops and the changes are small: a card reader taped beside the till, a menu that finally lists prices, a fan that works. The chairs stay. So does the noise.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Most of all, the coffee stays. Beans are still roasted dark with sugar and margarine, then brewed strong through cloth and poured from a height to cool it. The language stays too, and new customers are expected to learn it.'],
                ['type' => 'guide', 'title' => 'How to order at the counter', 'rows' => [
                    ['term' => 'Kopi', 'meaning' => 'Coffee with condensed milk'],
                    ['term' => 'Kopi-O', 'meaning' => 'Black, with sugar'],
                    ['term' => 'Kopi-O kosong', 'meaning' => 'Black, no sugar'],
                    ['term' => 'Kopi-C', 'meaning' => 'With evaporated milk and sugar'],
                    ['term' => 'Peng', 'meaning' => 'Iced. Add it to any order'],
                ]],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Where the younger owners do experiment, it tends to be at the edges. Farid’s sister added a nasi lemak on Saturdays, made to an aunt’s recipe, and sold out by ten. Mei Ling opened on Sunday evenings for the first time in the shop’s history, and found a crowd of teenagers doing homework under the fluorescent lights.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Who it’s for', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'There is a risk in all of this, and the owners know it. A restored kopitiam photographs well. Once a shop becomes a destination, the people who made it worth visiting can be priced out of their own table.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'A kopi-o at Lam Fatt still costs less than a bus ride. Mei Ling raised it once, by twenty sen, and lost sleep over it. She worries more about the weekend visitors who stay for an hour, order one drink and photograph the floor tiles.'],
                ['type' => 'pullquote', 'text' => 'A lawyer and a lorry driver share a table and nobody finds it strange.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“A kopitiam is the last place in town where a lawyer and a lorry driver share a table and nobody finds it strange,” she says. “If only the lawyers come, I’ve just opened a cafe.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Uncle Keong is less worried. He has watched the shop survive a flood, two recessions and the arrival of instant coffee. He takes the long view of tourists, as he does of most things.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Closing time', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'By two in the afternoon the shop is nearly empty. The kettle is cooling. Mei Ling has filmed eleven mornings now, and her notebook holds a recipe of sorts: weights, temperatures and a long column of crossed-out guesses.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She made a pot from it last week and gave him a cup without saying anything. He drank it slowly, looking out at the street.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“Almost,” he said, and went back to reading his newspaper.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She counts that as the best review the shop has had in years.'],
            ]],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug' => 'inside-the-ipoh-studio-reviving-hand-painted-cinema-posters',
        'desk' => 'Culture',
        'title' => 'Inside the Ipoh studio reviving hand-painted cinema posters',
        'deck' => 'A 78-year-old sign painter and his impatient apprentice are finding buyers for a craft the printing press retired decades ago.',
        'byline' => 'By the Meridian culture desk',
        'long_read' => false,
        'sections' => [
            ['name' => 'Eyes first', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Chan Kok Wai always starts with the eyes. On a hot Thursday above a hardware shop in Ipoh’s old town, he is working on a pair of them, each the size of a dinner plate, on a plywood board that takes up most of the wall. The rest of the face is still a pencil grid. The eyes already look annoyed with you.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“If the eyes are wrong, stop and go home,” he says. “The rest is only colouring.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Chan is 78. From the late 1960s until the early 1990s, he painted the enormous hoardings that hung above Ipoh’s cinemas: heroes with impossible jaws, heroines mid-gasp, and villains lit from below in shades of green that do not occur in nature. Then printed vinyl came along, and within a few years nobody needed him. He painted shop signs for a while, then lorry doors, and then he stopped.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Three years ago, a young woman knocked on his door and asked him to teach her. He said no. She came back the following week with a packet of char kuey teow and asked again. The studio that now occupies the upper floor, Lukisan Layar, is the outcome of that negotiation.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'The hoarding years', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In his prime, Chan worked in a shed behind a cinema that has since become a furniture showroom. There were five painters. Films changed every week or two, and a new hoarding would be due on Wednesday night, whatever had happened on Tuesday. Boards were painted in sections, carried over on a lorry, and bolted together in the dark.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The distributors sent publicity stills, which were often too small and sometimes of the wrong film. So the painters improvised. A hero’s moustache could be improved. Crowd scenes were added where the budget had not run to crowds. Chan once painted a tiger into a romantic drama, because the cinema manager felt that attendance needed help.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“There was no tiger in the story,” he says. “Nobody complained. They were only disappointed.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Raju Subramaniam, 69, lettered the titles in those days, in Malay, Chinese, Tamil and English, often on the same board. He comes into the studio twice a week now, officially to help and mainly to disagree. He can still throw a line of Tamil script freehand along a pencil rule without looking as if he is paying attention. The boards from those years are almost all gone. Paint was expensive and plywood more so, so each hoarding was scraped back and painted over for the following week.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Chan owns two photographs of his own work. Both are slightly out of focus, and one has a bus in it.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'The grid', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Nurul Izzati Hamdan, 26, studied graphic design and spent a year laying out banners for a supermarket chain’s promotions. She found one of Chan’s old lorry doors at a scrapyard in Menglembu, tracked down the name of the man who had painted it, and turned up at his door.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The method she has been learning is old and unforgiving. A photograph is ruled into small squares, and the board is ruled into matching big ones. The painter copies one square at a time, so that the eye is looking at shapes and has no chance to get ideas about faces. Colours are mixed in old condensed milk tins from enamel and house paint. Skin takes seven tones, and shadow goes in first.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'For her first year, she was allowed to paint backgrounds and nothing else. For most of the second, she painted ears.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“I have a degree,” she says. “I’ve painted maybe four hundred ears. He looks at them and says ‘ear’, and that’s the good review.”'],
                ['type' => 'pullquote', 'text' => 'If the eyes are wrong, stop and go home.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Chan’s view is that she is improving at a satisfactory pace and talks too much. His hands now shake a little before lunch, so he works in the mornings and leaves the long, straight edges to her.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'New customers', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'There is no longer a cinema in Malaysia that needs a 20-foot painted hoarding on a Wednesday. So Nurul has gone looking for other people who might want one. The largest group, to Chan’s continuing amazement, is couples getting married.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'For about RM3,800, Lukisan Layar will paint a bride and groom as the stars of a film that was never made, with a title, a tagline and supporting cast of relatives. One accountant from Taiping is depicted leaping from an exploding boat. A dentist from Seremban and her husband appear beneath the words “This Time, It’s Flossing.” Requests to look thinner are frequent, and the studio’s policy is to ask how much thinner, and then do about half.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Cafes commission them. So do a few collectors, and a film society in Kuala Lumpur, which ordered a set of three for a season of old Malay musicals.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The sums are modest. The studio does about 30 pieces in a year, each taking two to three weeks, and it clears enough to pay Nurul a wage, buy the paint and keep Raju in teh tarik. Nobody involved thinks that this is an industry. Nurul calls it “a very large hobby with an invoice book.”'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Wet paint', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'There are people who find all this a little sad: an art made for the crowd, now sold as a joke at wedding receptions. Chan doesn’t. He spent 25 years making paintings that lasted a fortnight, and he is rather pleased that someone is prepared to hang one in a house.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'What troubles him is the arithmetic of apprentices. Learning this properly takes about five years, and Nurul is the only person who has asked in the last 30. She has started a Saturday class for secondary school students, and six turned up to the first one. Three came back. One of them, a quiet 15-year-old from Falim, has been told that his ears show promise.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'By late afternoon, the light through the back window has turned the colour of weak tea, and Chan has washed his brushes. The board on the wall now has a face, or most of one. Nurul has been given the jawline, which is the first time he has let her near a jaw.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'He inspects it from across the room, with his glasses pushed up on his forehead, for what feels like a long time. Then he picks up his bag.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“Tomorrow,” he says, “we fix it.” She has decided that this is praise.'],
            ]],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug' => 'kampung-to-cloud-the-quiet-digitisation-of-rural-sabah',
        'desk' => 'Long read',
        'title' => 'Kampung to cloud: the quiet digitisation of rural Sabah',
        'deck' => 'Over six months in three villages, fibre, e-wallets and home-grown delivery networks arrived faster, messier and with more hope than the statistics suggest.',
        'byline' => 'By Joanna Gimbang, East Malaysia bureau',
        'long_read' => true,
        'sections' => [
            ['name' => 'The Johor order', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The order comes in at 6.40 on a wet Monday morning, while the rice is still on. Odah Lingkapan, 67, does not stop stirring. Her daughter reads it out from the phone: six jars of tuhau paste, the hot version, to a nurse in Johor Bahru who was born not far from here and who says, in a voice note, that the ones she bought last month made her cry in a good way.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Tuhau is a wild ginger that grows in the hills of northern Sabah. It smells powerfully of something between a forest and an argument. Pounded with chilli, salt and lime, it is the taste of home for a great many Sabahans, and a matter of some suspicion to everyone else. Odah has been making it in this kitchen, in Kampung Rosok Ulu in the district of Kota Marudu, for most of her life.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Until this year she sold it at the Sunday market, or gave it away. Her daughter Rosnah, 43, grows rubber and a little hill padi on the family’s land, and she handles what she describes as head office. Head office is a phone with a cracked corner, an exercise book and a shelf of washed jars.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“We used to ride two hours to the town bank. Now my mother sells her tuhau paste to buyers in Johor from her kitchen.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Rosnah says this flatly, while looking for a lid. She has said it a few times now, to relatives, to a visiting officer, and to a man who came to sell her solar panels. She doesn’t seem to find it remarkable. Her mother, when asked, says that the Johor people should learn to eat it with less rice.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Between April and October, the East Malaysia bureau kept returning to three Sabah villages as high-speed fibre, digital payments and delivery networks run by the villagers themselves turned up, in that order, or the reverse, or all at once. We went expecting a tidy story about connectivity. What we found was a lot of people on motorbikes, a lot of arguing about passwords, some real money, and a sense, which was hard to write down and which we kept running into, that something had come loose in a good way.'],
                ['type' => 'pullquote', 'text' => 'Now my mother sells her tuhau paste to buyers in Johor from her kitchen.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Three villages', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Kampung Rosok Ulu has about 90 households strung along a laterite road, 40 minutes past the last stretch of tar. People here are mostly Dusun, mostly smallholders, and mostly related to one another in ways that take an afternoon to explain. The nearest bank is in Kota Marudu town. In the rainy season, the two-hour ride Rosnah mentions could take rather longer, and sometimes ended with pushing.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Kampung Tobou Baru sits high in the interior hills, in a fold of land near Tambunan where the mornings are cold enough for jackets and the mobile signal, until recently, could only be found by standing on one particular water tank. The village has 60 households, a small primary school and a strong tradition of sending its children somewhere else. Melvin Sintian, 38, the deputy head of the school, has taught for 14 years. Of the children he taught in his first five years, he reckons fewer than one in ten still lives there.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Kampung Selangkir Laut is on the coast, on the Pitas side of the Kudat peninsula, with houses on stilts over the tidal flats and a jetty that has been about to be repaired for some years. About 120 families live here, Bajau and Rungus for the most part, fishing, drying fish and gathering shellfish. The road arrives eventually. Most things, including most gossip, arrive by boat.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'They have little in common beyond being places that national plans describe as “rural and remote”, a phrase that nobody in them uses. All three got fibre within about the same year, under the sort of rollout that gets announced from a stage. On the page, they look like three identical dots turning from grey to green.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'On the ground, they were three very different experiments. In Rosok Ulu the change came through a kitchen. In Tobou Baru it came through a school. In Selangkir Laut it came through a woman selling ice.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'The cable arrives', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The cable came to Tobou Baru on the back of a contractor’s lorry in April, and then stopped for five weeks, because the lorry could not get round the last bend. The crew, four men from Keningau and a supervisor who was permanently on the phone, ended up hauling drums of fibre up the hill on a borrowed tractor and then, for the final stretch, by hand. Villagers who had come to watch ended up helping. A number of them were then paid, a development that increased the number of people coming to watch.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Jeffrey Majalin, 52, a farmer who knows every ridge in the area, was hired as a guide and ended up in effect running the route. He moved the line three times, once to avoid a grave and twice to avoid his brother-in-law’s durian trees. “The plan was drawn by a person who has seen a map,” he says, kindly.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'On a Saturday in May, the crew lit up a small cabinet outside the community hall. Cikgu Melvin ran a speed test on his phone while about 40 people stood around him. The number that came up meant nothing to anybody, including him. But he opened a video, in high definition, and it played without stopping, on a hillside where people had been standing on a water tank to send a text message. Somebody’s grandmother asked if it could show the football. It could. The hall stayed open until midnight.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Connecting the village and connecting the houses turned out to be two separate events. The line stopped at the hall and the school. Home connections carried a monthly fee that many families looked at for a long time before saying nothing. So the hall’s Wi-Fi became the village’s Wi-Fi, and the steps of the hall became its busiest address. In the evenings you could count 25 people sitting on them with their faces lit from below, like a very peaceful religious movement.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Rosok Ulu’s connection arrived at about the same time, and with less ceremony. A crew went past. A week later a neighbour with a small sundry shop had a router, and began to sell access in bundles of a few ringgit, written in a notebook, with passwords she changes when she is cross. Selangkir Laut’s fibre stopped at the shore end of the village and left the houses over the water to work out their own arrangements, which they did, with a long run of outdoor cable tied to the walkway rails and a certain amount of faith.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The neighbour is Lorna Kimin, 49, and she has become, without anyone voting on it, the most closely watched businesswoman in the district. She charges RM3 for a day and RM15 for a week. Regulars can pay at the end of the month. People who have offended her tend to find the signal weak. She says that this is a technical matter relating to the rain.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'None of this matched the diagram. All of it, as far as anyone there was concerned, worked.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Money without tellers', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Sitti Hamidah, 46, sells ice from a chest freezer at the end of the jetty in Selangkir Laut, and she may be the most important financial institution in the village. Boats need ice before they leave. They have rarely been paid at that point, so for 20 years she has run a credit system out of her head and a school notebook.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In June, her nephew, home from Kota Kinabalu for the school holidays, printed a QR code, laminated it and tied it to the freezer with fishing line. She thought this was ridiculous. The following week, a buyer of dried anchovies from Kudat paid her with it, because he had run out of cash, and the money was in her phone before he had finished complaining about the road.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“I looked at it for a long time,” she says. “Nobody had to count anything. Nobody had to say ‘next week’.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'By August there were laminated codes on boats, on the walkway, and on a bucket belonging to a woman who sells cockles. Fishermen’s wives had begun to take payment directly from buyers, which changed a number of conversations at home. Middlemen, who for a long time had been the only route for cash and credit, found that sellers could now see prices in Kudat and Kota Kinabalu on the same morning. One buyer of salted fish told us, with feeling, that the village had “become very educated”.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The freedom had a cost, and it came quickly. In September, Hamidah’s neighbour, Halimah Jumat, a widow of 58, received a phone call from a polite young man who said he was from her bank, and who needed a six-digit number to protect her account. She lost RM1,400, which was most of what she had. She found out from the same phone, on the walkway, with several people watching.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The village’s response was faster than any awareness campaign. Hamidah called a meeting under the house next to the jetty, attended by about 30 women, at which it was resolved that nobody would read a number out to anybody without first showing the phone to somebody else. It became known as the two-auntie rule. As a piece of security design it is hard to fault. Halimah’s money did not come back. She runs the sign-up sheet for the next meeting, and she tells the story herself, every time, with the amount.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Rosnah’s own first digital sale went wrong in a minor way. A customer in Penampang sent RM36 to the wrong number, which belonged to a man in Beaufort. He passed it on, after some discussion, with a note wishing her well and asking what tuhau was. He has since ordered two jars. She keeps this story for people who tell her that the internet has made everyone colder.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Cash has not disappeared. Nearly everyone we met used both, and many used a digital wallet mainly as somewhere to keep money away from relatives. Odah in Rosok Ulu remains unconvinced of the whole business, and asks to have her share of the Johor orders in notes, which she keeps in a biscuit tin and counts in front of her daughter, slowly, as a form of audit.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Who carries it', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'A jar sold in a kitchen in Kota Marudu is still a jar in a kitchen in Kota Marudu. The large courier firms will deliver as far as the town, more or less, and their maps get vague after that. Buyers in the villages found that parcels were left at a shop in town for collection, sometimes for weeks. Sellers found that the last 40 kilometres cost more trouble than the following 2,000.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Alfred Gunsalam, 35, Rosnah’s cousin, owned a pickup and had a grievance. He had worked for several years as a driver in Kota Kinabalu and came home in 2024 to look after his father. Early this year, he started collecting parcels from town on Tuesdays and Fridays, for a small fee, and taking outgoing orders back the other way. By May he had recruited two friends with motorbikes for the side roads, and a school-leaver named Clara, 19, to keep the records. They called it Hantar Sama-Sama, which translates roughly as “we deliver together” and which Alfred admits was the first name anyone suggested.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Clara’s spreadsheet now covers 11 villages. It has a column for the parcel, a column for the money owed, and a column headed “Notes” which is the most interesting document we saw in six months. Entries include “dog at house, leave with neighbour”, “do not tell husband about this parcel” and, on one occasion, “jar exploded on school books, have apologised to Cikgu”.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The exploding jar was educational. Tuhau paste ferments in a warm truck. After the incident, Rosnah changed her recipe slightly, loosened her lids before packing and began wrapping every jar in newspaper, then plastic, then a stern handwritten note. Complaints from Johor fell sharply.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Selangkir Laut developed its own network on the water. Boats going to Kudat for supplies now carry parcels for a small charge, coordinated through a group chat run by the boat owners’ wives, and a share of the fee goes towards fixing the jetty, a fund which has so far bought one plank. In Tobou Baru, where few people are selling anything yet, the traffic runs the other way. Hill farmers there have begun ordering seed, spare parts and a surprising quantity of hair dye. The parcels go up the hill with the man who delivers gas cylinders.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The economics are simple enough to fit on the back of Clara’s hand, and in the early weeks they did. A small parcel costs RM4 from town to the door. A trip to a farm beyond the river is RM6, payable in cash, by wallet or occasionally in pineapples. Clara has added a column for the pineapples. After three months she worked out that the run to the far end of the district lost money every single week. Alfred read the figures, nodded and kept going, because three old people out there get their medicine that way.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“The online shop people think the last step is the door,” Alfred says. “There is no door. There is a river and then there is Auntie Doris, who is at the farm until four.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'He does not make much. After fuel and tyres, he reckons that he clears about RM1,500 in a good month, and the pickup is getting old. He has been approached by two people from Kota Kinabalu who would like to invest in his “logistics platform”. He told them that it was a pickup. They said that they understood, and asked if he had a deck.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'What broke', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In the second week of July a slope gave way above the road to Tobou Baru, and it took a section of cable with it. The village was offline for 19 days. Cikgu Melvin had by that point moved a good part of his teaching materials online, and he went back to the photocopier, which had also broken, and then to the blackboard, which had not. People returned, a little sheepishly, to the water tank.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Everyone we spoke to in all three villages had stories like this. Power cuts take the router down with them. A long spell of bad weather can stop the delivery run and leave the jars sitting. The neighbour who sells access in Rosok Ulu went to a wedding in Ranau for a week, and took the password with her.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Control of the connection has turned out to be a small, fierce kind of politics. In Tobou Baru, the password for the hall’s Wi-Fi was first held by the village committee, and then, following a disagreement over who had been watching what on it, by Cikgu Melvin, who did not want the job. It is now changed monthly and announced after church. Some families were sure that they were being told last. They may have been.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Success made its own trouble. Word got round about the tuhau. By August, four other households along the road in Rosok Ulu were selling the paste online, and one of them undercut Odah by a ringgit a jar. The resulting silence between two kitchens lasted about three weeks and was broken only by a funeral. Quality varies. Labelling, on the whole, is an aspiration. Rosnah has begun to wonder what a food safety officer would make of the shelf of washed jars, and she has asked us not to overdo the description of it.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Then there are those who are simply not in the picture. Older people without smartphones depend on someone younger to do it for them, and that someone is not always patient. People on the steep end of the village in Rosok Ulu get no signal from the neighbour’s router. At Selangkir Laut, the families at the far end of the walkway are the poorest and have the worst connection, as the cable’s reach and the village’s economic order happen to coincide. We met one father who sold his bicycle to buy his daughter a second-hand phone for school, and who could not then afford the data.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The forms came as a surprise. Applications for aid, boat licences and small-trader registrations are increasingly done online, and the hall steps and the jetty have become informal service counters. Sitti Hamidah’s nephew fills them in for nothing, for now. In Rosok Ulu, Clara helps on Fridays. She says that about half the job is finding out somebody’s identity card number, and the other half is explaining why the website has logged them out again.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Anyone telling a story of smooth uplift has not spent much time on the hall steps. People in these villages are well aware of the gaps. They have also, mostly, stopped waiting for somebody else to come and close them.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'Who came home', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Vanessa Gaban, 24, moved back to Tobou Baru from Kuala Lumpur in June. She was working in customer support for a travel company there, spending most of her salary on a shared room in Cheras. When the fibre arrived, she asked her manager if she could work from home, not expecting much. Her manager, who had never heard of Tobou Baru, agreed.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She now takes calls from a converted store room at the back of her parents’ house, on a connection that she pays for herself. Customers in Europe complain to her about their hotel rooms. Outside the window, her father’s buffalo looks in. She has trained herself not to react when it breathes on the glass during a call.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“My salary is the same as before,” she says. “But now it’s rich. In KL, it was only just enough. Here, I’m the wealthiest person I know, and I still have to help with the padi.”'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She is one of only a handful. We should be careful here, as the villages themselves are. Clara in Rosok Ulu stayed because of the job at Hantar Sama-Sama, though she is saving to study in Kota Kinabalu. A young man in Selangkir Laut learned to repair phone screens from online videos and now has a queue. Three of Cikgu Melvin’s former students have returned for good, and he has noticed.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Against that, the same connection also makes it far easier to leave. Teenagers in all three villages can now see what jobs exist and what they pay, in far greater detail than any careers talk. One of Cikgu Melvin’s best students, a 16-year-old with a gift for mathematics, has spent the year looking up engineering courses in Johor and Penang. Her teacher is delighted and depressed in about equal measure.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'For the school, the change has been easier to measure, at least informally. Children who once shared one old textbook between three can look things up. Cikgu Melvin’s Year 6 pupils recorded their grandparents telling folk stories in Dusun, then typed them up and posted them on the school’s page. One of the stories received comments from Sabahans in Perth, Brunei and Kajang. An old man who had not been asked anything by anyone in some time has started dressing up for his interviews.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Health came up more often than we expected. The clinic nearest Tobou Baru is an hour down the hill. Agnes Moitin, 61, who has diabetes, now sends a photograph of her blood sugar readings every Sunday from the hall steps to her niece, a pharmacy assistant in Keningau. The niece is blunt about the results. Agnes says that she has cut down on sweet tea, mainly so that she does not have to hear about it.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Nobody here is claiming that the outflow from rural Sabah has ended. What seems to have ended, for a few people at least, is the idea that going away is the only way of getting on.'],
            ]],
            ['name' => 'What the tables miss', 'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'The national statistics will get to these three villages eventually, in the form of a coverage percentage. They will record premises passed and subscriptions active, and from them, Rosok Ulu will appear to be doing rather badly, given that about a quarter of its homes have a line of their own. The tables have no column for a neighbour with a notebook, or for the hall steps, or for a cable tied to a walkway with fishing line. They will not count the two-auntie rule.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'They will not pick up the pace of it either. The official language of digital inclusion is measured and sequential, full of phases and pillars. What we saw over six months was faster than that and a good deal less orderly. People skipped steps. A woman who had never had a bank card went straight to a QR code on a freezer. A delivery network came into being because one man was annoyed. Much of it was improvised, some of it will fail, and a certain amount of it would alarm a regulator.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'It was also, over and over, more hopeful than we had expected to find. This is an uncomfortable thing for a reporter to write down, since hope doesn’t have a source and can’t be checked. But we kept meeting people who had started, this year, to make plans that assumed a next year. Sitti Hamidah wants a second freezer. Alfred wants a newer pickup and has started to use the word “route”. Halimah, who lost her savings, has asked to learn how to check her own balance, and now does so twice a day.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'On our last visit to Rosok Ulu, in the middle of October, it rained so hard that the road had turned the colour of milky tea, and the Friday pickup was running late. Rosnah had 14 jars ready, wrapped and labelled, on a table by the door. One of them was going to Sandakan, three to Kuala Lumpur, and ten to Johor, where the nurse has apparently been telling her colleagues.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'Rosnah now earns more from the paste in a good month than from the rubber. She said so carefully, since rubber prices can change and so can people’s taste for wild ginger. She has opened an account that she can check on her phone. She has not been to the town bank since March. She mentioned that she misses the fried bananas near the bank, which is the only thing about that journey that anyone has ever been heard to miss.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'In the kitchen, her mother had started on another batch. Odah had by now heard a fair amount about what was going on: the fibre, the buyers, the reporter from the city writing it all down. She pounded the tuhau for a while without comment. We asked her what she made of it all, of a jar leaving her kitchen for the far end of the peninsula on the say-so of a telephone.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => 'She considered the question, tasted the paste, and added more salt.'],
                ['type' => 'p', 'text' => '“Tell them it’s RM18,” she said. “Tell them the next batch is RM20.”'],
            ]],
        ],
    ],
];
